note as N1
  is_busy() per tool (true = something is in progress that Cancel would commit or discard)
  StrokeTool: stroke is Some | ShapeSelectTool: drag is Some | SelectPolygonTool: vertices is not empty
  MoveTool: drag is Some | FillTool: always false | PickTool: always false
  overlay(view) is a pure read (called every egui frame while painting); it never mutates the tool.
  Paint / Erase return BrushFootprint(op.footprint(hover, view)) while the pointer is over the page, so the user sees which cells a click would touch.
end note

note as N2
  Selection semantics
  - Not part of the project file. NOT recorded by the history, except together with the two operations that move it (Move cells,
    Paste / Import as layer): those store a SelectionChange{ before, after } next to their command (see diagram 05).
    It persists across frame and layer switches (a page-level mask shared by all layers).
  - permits(c) = is_empty() || contains(c). Paint, Erase, Fill and "Clear selection" call permits() before writing a cell, so with an
    active selection the edits are clipped to it; with no selection the whole page is editable.
  - apply(cells, Replace): clear(), then insert all.  apply(cells, Add): insert all.  apply(cells, Subtract): remove all.
  - invert(): complement inside the page. Does nothing on an empty selection (the menu item is disabled). When the complement is empty
    (select all, then invert) the selection is empty again = whole page editable.
  - translate(dx, dy) -> bool: shifts the mask, returns false and changes nothing when a selected cell would leave the page
    (the Move tool clamps its offset first, so this never happens in practice).
  - restore(other): replace the mask and its size (undo / redo of a SelectionChange).  resize(spec): grow the mask, new cells unselected.
  - Every mutation bumps revision; the canvas caches boundary_edges() per revision.
  - Keyboard (handled by EditorScreen, not by a tool): Ctrl+A = select_all(), Ctrl+D / Esc (no tool busy) = clear(), Ctrl+Shift+I = invert(),
    Delete = clear the contents of every selected cell of the ACTIVE layer (both planes) as ONE command (see 14c).
end note

note as N3
  Event routing rule (CanvasView -> ToolRegistry.dispatch)
  - Pointer position is converted to a cell with floor(). If it is outside the page:
      * PointerDown outside the page is dropped (tool never sees it).
      * PointerMove / PointerUp outside the page send cell = None; tools then use the LAST valid cell
        (stroke tools skip the segment, shape tools clamp via CanvasSpec::clamp, the Move tool keeps its offset).
  - PointerMove is sent for EVERY pointer move over the canvas (button up or down), so tools can show hover previews.
  - Only the primary (left) button reaches tools. Middle button / Space+drag = pan (see ViewportController).
  - While playback is Playing the CanvasView sends NO tool events.
  - Switching tool = ToolRegistry.switch_to(): old.on_event(Deactivate) then new.on_event(Activate).
  - Deactivate / Cancel semantics: StrokeTool commits what was painted so far; MoveTool rolls the drag back (nothing is moved);
    ShapeSelectTool and SelectPolygonTool discard the preview (selection unchanged).
  - Write guard (Paint, Erase, Fill, Move-contents): they only ever write into the ACTIVE layer of the selected frame, and only when
    that layer is visible. On a hidden layer the event is Consumed, nothing changes, the status bar says "Layer is hidden".
  - PickTool never writes: it returns ToolResponse::Picked(colours) and the caller (CanvasView -> EditorSession::apply_picked) updates the ToolOptions colours.
    It therefore works on hidden layers and ignores the selection.
  - There is no text input any more: egui Event::Text is not translated into tool events (characters are typed only in the Brush editor).
end note

note as N4
  PatchBuilder - records the edits of ONE layer of ONE frame
  write_ink(project, c, v) / write_fill(project, c, v):
  1. v = v.normalized(). old = current value of that plane at c (c outside the page -> return false).
  2. old == v -> return false (no-op, nothing recorded).
  3. first time c is touched in this plane: before[c] = old (never overwritten later).
  4. frame.set_ink / set_fill; after[c] = v; push c to the plane's `order` once.
  5. return true.
  clear(project, cells) = write_ink(EMPTY) + write_fill(TRANSPARENT) for every cell.
  blit(project, buf, offset) = the blit rule of CellBuffer (only non-empty source cells overwrite), cells translated by offset, off-page cells skipped.
  The cell is changed IMMEDIATELY (live feedback); history only receives the command at finish().
  finish(label): drop cells where before == after (per plane); nothing left -> None; else Some(CellPatchCommand{ frame_id, layer_id, ink_changes,
  fill_changes, label }). The tool then calls history.record_applied(cmd) - it does NOT apply again.
  rollback(project): write every `before` value back (reverse order of `order`) and forget the changes (a cancelled Move drag).
end note

note as N5
  Stroke rules (Paint / Erase)
  - PointerMove(Some(c)) while idle: hover = Some(c) (drives the BrushFootprint overlay).
  - PointerDown(c): start StrokeState{ last = c, builder = PatchBuilder::new(frame id, active layer id) }, then op.stamp(c).
  - PointerMove(Some(c)) while a stroke is active: for every cell k in ShapeRasterizer::line(last, c) (excluding `last`, including c)
    call op.stamp(k). Then last = c. The brush stamp is repeated on every cell of the line, so fast mouse moves leave no gaps.
  - PointerMove(None): keep the stroke alive, do not change `last`.
  - PointerUp / Deactivate / Cancel: finish(op.label()) -> history.record_applied(cmd); stroke = None. One drag = ONE undo step.
  - The selection is checked per target cell inside stamp(), so a stroke may cross in and out of the selection.
end note

note as N6
  EraseOperation
  footprint(at) = square of erase_size x erase_size cells, anchor = (erase_size - 1) / 2 on both axes, clipped to the page.
  stamp(at): for every cell k of the footprint with selection.permits(k):
    planes.foreground -> write_ink(k, InkCell::EMPTY)       planes.background -> write_fill(k, Rgba::TRANSPARENT)
  label() = "Erase cells". With no plane switched on the tool does nothing.
end note

note as N7
  PaintOperation
  brush = brushes.get(options.brush). footprint(at) = every glyph cell of the stamp, placed so that stamp.anchor lands on `at`, clipped to the page.
  stamp(at): for every footprint cell k (stamp glyph ch) with selection.permits(k):
    planes.foreground -> write_ink(k, InkCell{ glyph: ch, color: options.ink_color })
    planes.background -> write_fill(k, options.fill_color)
  A stamp cell that is empty (None) leaves the page cell untouched in BOTH planes.
  Painting REPLACES the cell value - colours are mixed between layers, never inside a plane.
  label() = "Paint cells". With no plane switched on the tool does nothing.
end note

note as N8
  Brushes
  - A brush is a stamp: up to 8 x 8 cells, each cell = one glyph or empty (None = "do not touch this cell"), plus an anchor cell that
    lands on the pointer. A single-character brush is a 1 x 1 stamp.
  - Built-in brushes (ids 1..999, read-only): 1 x 1 stamps of  .  :  -  =  +  *  #  %  @  and the blocks U+2588 U+2593 U+2592 U+2591.
    Built-ins whose glyph is missing from the font are hidden in the picker (ascii_app asks GlyphSource::contains).
  - Custom brushes are created / edited / deleted in the Brush editor, the ONLY place where characters are typed (diagram 13b).
    Ids start at 1000. Stored per user in <config_dir>/AsciiPaint/brushes.json (BrushStore, diagram 07), NOT inside .aspr files.
  - validate(): name 1..=32 chars and unique (case-insensitive), width and height 1..=8, at least one glyph,
    anchor inside the stamp AND on a glyph cell, every glyph a printable Unicode scalar (' ' is allowed).
  - fill_glyph() = the glyph under the anchor. This is the "char" the Fill tool uses.
  - Deleting the active brush makes options.brush = BrushLibrary::default_id() (the '#' brush).
end note

note as N9
  Fill rules (works on the ACTIVE layer only, never reads another layer)
  - PointerDown(c): the active layer must be visible. If the selection is non-empty and !selection.contains(c) -> ignored.
  - EVERY plane switched on in options.planes is filled independently - the boundary of a fill is decided by that plane alone:
      Foreground: region = FloodFill::region(layer, c, Foreground, &selection) = the connected cells whose InkCell equals the seed's InkCell
                  (glyph AND colour). Every cell gets InkCell{ glyph: brush.fill_glyph(), color: options.ink_color }.
      Background: region = FloodFill::region(layer, c, Background, &selection) = the connected cells whose fill colour equals the seed's.
                  Every cell gets options.fill_color.
    Both planes on = two separate regions written through ONE PatchBuilder (one undo step), label "Fill cells".
  - If the seed already holds the target value in a plane, that plane's region is empty (nothing to do). Nothing written -> nothing recorded.
  - Example: fill an empty area with '#' in red (foreground), then fill the same area's background blue. The two fills never decide
    each other's boundary.
end note

note as N10
  Rect / Ellipse select rules
  - PointerDown(c): drag = DragState{ anchor: c, current: c, mode: options.mode_for(mods) } (Shift = Add, Alt = Subtract, else the top bar mode).
  - PointerMove(Some(c)): drag.current = c.  PointerMove(None): drag.current = clamp(last pointer position).
  - overlay(): RectPreview / EllipsePreview of CellRect::from_corners(anchor, current) (nothing is selected yet).
  - PointerUp: cells = shape.rasterize(anchor, current); selection.apply(cells, drag.mode); drag = None.
    A click without moving selects the single cell under the cursor (a 1x1 rectangle / ellipse).
  - Cancel / Escape during drag: drag = None, selection unchanged.
end note

note as N11
  Polygon select rules
  - PointerDown(c): if vertices is empty, mode = options.mode_for(mods). If c equals the LAST vertex ignore it
      (this also absorbs the 2nd click of a double-click). If c equals vertices[0]: close() when vertices.len() >= 3,
      otherwise ignore it. Otherwise push c.
  - PointerMove(Some(c)): hover = Some(c) (rubber-band edge preview).
  - DoubleClick / Key(Enter): close().
  - Key(Backspace): pop last vertex. If empty the tool is idle again.
  - Key(Escape) / Cancel / Deactivate: vertices.clear() (selection unchanged).
  - close(): if vertices.len() < 3 -> discard and clear. Else
      cells = ShapeRasterizer::polygon(vertices); selection.apply(cells, mode); vertices.clear().
end note

note as N12
  Move rules (needs a non-empty selection, options.move_mode = Contents or OutlineOnly)
  - PointerDown(c): ignored unless selection.contains(c). Otherwise drag = MoveDrag{ anchor: c, offset: (0, 0), selection_before: selection.clone() }.
      Contents    : the active layer must be visible. floating = Arc::new(CellBuffer::extract(layer, selection)); builder = PatchBuilder::new(..);
                    builder.clear(project, selected cells) - the layer shows a hole right away.
      OutlineOnly : no buffer, no builder.
  - PointerMove(Some(c)): offset = (c - anchor) clamped so that selection.bounds() shifted by offset stays inside the page. PointerMove(None): offset unchanged.
    overlay() = MovePreview{ floating, offset }: the renderer draws the floating cells and the shifted selection outline.
  - PointerUp:
      offset == (0, 0) : Contents -> builder.rollback() (nothing recorded). OutlineOnly -> nothing.
      Contents         : builder.blit(project, floating, offset); selection.translate(offset); cmd = builder.finish("Move cells");
                         history.record_applied_with_selection(cmd, SelectionChange{ before: selection_before, after: selection.clone() }).
      OutlineOnly      : selection.translate(offset) only (selection changes are not undoable).
  - Key(Arrow*) with no drag and a non-empty selection: one nudge by 1 cell (Shift = 10 cells), executed exactly like PointerUp with that offset
    (clamped to the page). Each key press is one undo step.
  - Cancel / Escape / Deactivate during a drag: Contents -> builder.rollback(). drag = None. Selection unchanged.
  - Moved cells overwrite the destination only where the moved cell has content (blit rule of CellBuffer). The cells the move left behind are empty.
end note

note as N13
  ToolOptions = everything the top bar edits. One value per editor session, not saved in the project.
  - brush + planes: Paint (stamp and planes), Fill (fill glyph and planes), Erase (planes). ink_color + fill_color: Paint and Fill.
  - erase_size 1..=16: Erase only.
  - selection_mode: the Replace / Add / Subtract buttons of the three select tools.
    mode_for(mods) = Shift -> Add, Alt -> Subtract, otherwise options.selection_mode. A held modifier always wins over the button.
  - move_mode: Contents (default) or OutlineOnly.
  - pick_source: Merged (default) or ActiveLayer. planes also tells the Pick tool WHICH colour to pick (Foreground -> ink_color, Background -> fill_color).
  - default(): brush '#', planes = { Foreground }, ink_color = opaque white, fill_color = opaque #808080, erase_size = 1, Replace, Contents, Merged.
end note

note as N14
  Pick rules (colour picker / eyedropper). Never writes into the project: not undoable, not dirty-making.
  - PointerMove(Some(c)): hover = Some(c). overlay() = BrushFootprint([c]), a one-cell outline of the cell that would be sampled.
  - PointerDown(c), and PointerMove(Some(c)) while the button is held (drag to scrub): sample cell c and return
    ToolResponse::Picked(PickedColors{ ink, fill }) according to the paint mode in options.planes:
      planes.foreground -> ink  = the character colour at c
      planes.background -> fill = the background colour at c
    options.pick_source decides where the colours come from:
      Merged      : cell = Compositor::flatten_cell(selected frame, c). ink = cell.ink when cell.glyph != ' ', fill = cell.fill when cell.fill.a > 0.
                    This is the colour mix the user SEES (layer opacity and alpha mixing included; the frame background is not part of fill).
      ActiveLayer : the active layer's own cell. ink = ink.color when its glyph != ' ', fill = fill colour when its alpha > 0. Also works on a hidden layer.
  - A plane with nothing at c (no character, or a fully transparent background) yields None for that plane: that colour stays as it was and the status bar
    says "No character here" / "No background here". No plane switched on = nothing is picked.
  - Only colours are picked. The brush / glyph is never changed. The picked colour keeps its alpha.
  - EditorSession::apply_picked(p): ink -> set_ink_color, fill -> set_fill_color. The active selection is ignored (picking works anywhere on the page).
  - PointerUp / Cancel / Deactivate: nothing to commit.
end note

